# How do you win a won game?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "Why trade pieces, not pawns, when you are ahead? How do you take away the opponent's counterplay before you cash in?"
next: {"title":"How do you save a lost game?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/27-how-do-you-save-a-lost-game.cast"}

## Warm-up: count, then promote
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 3r2k1/pp3ppp/4p3/3r4/8/2N5/PP2BPPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxd5, exd5]}
      - {fen: 8/4k3/PP6/8/4b3/8/7K/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [b7, Bxb7, axb7], arrows: [{from: e4, to: a8}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: how do you win a game you're already winning? First, two warm-ups: counting, and last time's endings.
    draw position

B: Black's rook on d5: I attack it twice, he guards it twice.
    highlight piece_c3 piece_d1

B: So I take with the rook, and the knight takes back.
    point at.ref piece_d1 gesture circle

A: Rook first, and the pawn takes it. Then his rook on d8 takes your knight. Count the points, not just the men.
    highlight piece_e6 piece_d8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Send the cheapest attacker in first.","Your knight on c3."] themes ["counting"] voice a
        right: Knight takes d5, and the pawn takes back: a knight for a rook. Then stop, because his rook on d8 guards d5 again.
        wrong: Rook first gives five for five, and then you lose the knight. Which attacker costs least?

B: A rook for a knight, and I stop: his rook still guards d5.
    highlight piece_d8

A: Now an ending from last time: two pawns against a bishop.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: The bishop guards b7 and a8. Push to a7, and it just waits.
    draw arrow_0

A: There's a better push.
    highlight piece_b6

    erase arrow_0

    pause 0.3

    ask "Make a queen." widget chess line true hints ["Give the bishop a pawn to take.","The pawn on b6."] themes ["minor piece ending","decoy"] voice a
        right: Pawn to b7. If the bishop takes, the a-pawn takes back, and nothing covers b8; if it doesn't, b8 makes a queen.
        wrong: Pushing to a7 lets the bishop wait. Which push forces it to take?

B: One bishop can't stop both pawns here.
    highlight piece_b7

A: Both puzzles turn on what comes off the board. Today: once you're ahead, what should come off?
    fade position to 0.35

## Trade pieces, then shut the door
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 3r4/1p2kppp/p7/8/8/5N2/PP3PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxd8, Kxd8]}
      - {fen: 8/8/8/4k3/8/2KN4/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: []}
      - {fen: 4r1k1/pp3ppp/8/3N4/8/8/P1P2PPP/1R4K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxb7]}
      - {fen: 4r1k1/pR3ppp/8/3N4/8/8/P1P2PPP/6K1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Re1#], flip: true}
      - {fen: 4r1k1/pp3ppp/8/3N4/8/8/P1P2PPP/1R4K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [h3, b6], highlights: [h2]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    safety: 0
sources:
    - id: draw
      title: Draw (chess)
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 'King and knight versus king, and king and bishop versus king, are insufficient material: no sequence of legal moves can lead to checkmate, so the game is drawn.'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Draw_(chess)

    card title "Trade pieces, then shut the door"

A: Now the lesson. White is a knight up here, and it's White's move.
    draw position

B: I'd keep the rooks on. Trades make games drawish, and I want to attack.
    highlight piece_d1 piece_d8

A: Count the pieces, not the pawns: rook and knight against a rook. Eight against five.
    text t85 "8 v 5" x 905 y 400 font_size 44

A: Trade the rooks,
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 2.4

    erase t85

A: and it's three against nothing.
    text t30 "3 v 0" x 905 y 400 font_size 44

A: Your extra knight didn't change. His army shrank, so your extra piece counts for more.
    highlight piece_f3

B: And with fewer pieces, he has fewer ways to hurt me.
    highlight piece_d8

    erase t30

B: Then I'll trade everything. Pawns too.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: White is a knight up, and every pawn is gone. Who wins?
    highlight piece_d3

    pause 0.3

    quiz "White is a knight up, and every pawn is gone. Who wins?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 905 buttons_at.y 375 buttons_layout column say_question false voice a
        right: A draw: a king and knight alone can't mate a lone king.
        wrong: Try to picture the mate with only a king and a knight.
        * White
        * Black
        + A draw

A: Your pawns are what turn an extra piece into a win: the piece eats his pawns, or walks one of yours to a queen.
    highlight piece_d3

A: So the guide: trade pieces, keep pawns.
    animate position_shown 0 plies_shown 2 duration 0.6

A: A guide, not a law: a pawn trade that makes you a passed pawn can be right.
    highlight piece_a2 piece_b2 piece_f2 piece_g2 piece_h2

    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Second question: what can he still do to you? Here too, White is a knight up.
    highlight piece_d5 piece_e8

B: And the pawn on b7 is loose. I take it.
    animate safety 1 duration 1

A: The safety lights count men, not mates. First, ask his Three Questions.
    highlight piece_e8

A: Say you take it.
    animate plies_shown 1 safety 0 duration 1.4

A: We're on Black's side of the board now. Find his move.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Punish the grab." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first.","White's king has no air on the back rank."] themes ["back rank"] voice a
        right: Rook to e1, mate. White's king has no air, and nothing can cover e1.
        wrong: Look at every check, starting with the rook.

B: Mated, a knight up.
    highlight piece_g1

A: So first, take away his chance.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: A quiet move like h3
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.4

A: gives your king air.
    draw hl_h2

A: Now his checks are harmless, and b7 is a real threat: he spends his move saving it.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.4

B: Slower. But I can't lose it.
    highlight piece_h3

    erase hl_h2

A: When you're ahead, the job changes:
    highlight piece_d5 piece_b1

A: trade his pieces, keep your pawns, and shut his last door before you cash in.
    highlight piece_h3 piece_g1

## Your turn: finish it
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Four for four. Your opponents will start resigning earlier.
    good: Most of them. The misses come back until they stick.
    poor: Being ahead takes practice too. Try the misses again.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 6k1/3b1ppp/8/8/5B2/4N3/P1p2PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxc2]}
      - {fen: 4r1k1/pp3p1p/6p1/8/8/3Q1p1q/PP3P1P/RR4K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qf1]}
      - {fen: r3k3/5ppp/8/1N6/8/8/P4PPP/2R3K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nc7+, Kd7, Nxa8]}
      - {fen: 8/7p/8/r2k4/8/6P1/5PK1/7R w - - 0 1, moves: [Rh5+, Ke6, Rxa5]}
      - {fen: 8/8/8/8/2k5/1p5P/6K1/3R4 w - - 0 1}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you've seen both habits: your turn. Four positions where you're ahead, then a game against the computer.
    draw position

A: First: Black's last hope is the pawn on c2.
    highlight piece_c2

    pause 0.3

    ask "Deal with it." widget chess line true hints ["Captures first.","Your knight on e3."] themes ["conversion","defence"] voice a test conv
        right: Knight takes c2. The plain capture is right here: that pawn was his only chance.
        wrong: Which of your pieces can take the pawn right now?

B: Sometimes the plain capture is the answer.
    highlight piece_c2

A: Next: Black threatens mate on g2.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    highlight piece_h3 piece_f3 duration 1

    ask "Stop the mate." widget chess line true hints ["Guard g2, and offer something.","Your queen on d3."] themes ["defence","conversion"] voice a test conv
        right: Queen to f1 guards g2 and offers the queens. If he takes, his attack is gone; if he backs off, so is his threat.
        wrong: Queen takes f3 loses the queen to his. Which queen move guards g2?

B: So the trade was the defence.
    highlight piece_f1

A: Now a fork, from week two. He has one rook left.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A knight check.","Which square hits the king and the rook?"] themes ["fork","conversion"] voice a test conv
        right: Knight to c7, check: the king moves, and the knight takes the rook on a8. He has nothing left to fight with.
        wrong: Look for a knight check that also hits a8.

A: Last one: a skewer. Here you're only a pawn up.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["His king and rook share a rank.","A rook check along the fifth rank."] themes ["skewer","conversion"] voice a test conv
        right: Rook to h5, check: the king has to leave the fifth rank, and the rook takes a5.
        wrong: Check the king along the line it shares with his rook.

    hide position

A: You scored {conv.score} of {conv.total}.
    results test conv

    show position

    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Now finish one for real. You're a rook up, and his king is helping his pawn.
    highlight piece_b3 piece_c4

A: Watch the b-pawn first, then mate him within thirty moves.
    explore activity vs_computer goal.kind mate goal.within 30 store rookup

    hide position

A: Ahead, you win by taking his chances away: trade his pieces,
    text s_trade "trade pieces" x 500 y 500 font_size 72

A: keep your pawns,
    text s_keep "keep pawns" x 500 y 390 font_size 72

A: and check his threats before you grab.
    text s_check "check his threats" x 500 y 280 font_size 72

    erase s_trade s_keep s_check

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 480 font_size 110
    text hw2 "1st mistake?" x 500 y 320 font_size 84

## Where next
end_page: true

